State chemical quantities, composition, purity and throughput on a usable basis (@ic-quantities: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @industrial-chemistry, #source "../sources/Industrial-Chemical-Technology-I-NOUN.lingua#r_2026VMVXVJQGK3WTH1P17FE8T9", #source "../sources/LearnChemE-Process-Self-Study.lingua#r_4KM1THP1QA2SZ5JJ9E1HSMN529", #source "../sources/Chemical-Reactor-Analysis-Rawlings-Ekerdt.lingua#r_7YQAV27Y3J0B537Z6K5HDN8H0A", #industrial-chemistry "../../../../inquiry/metrology/foundations/Sampling-and-representativeness.lingua#r_0F0VEC42GPQPRZM0S7MXF7GAEH") { r_3VZAMY6D4469SV1678W5B3JS5T
**Read:** [Industrial Chemical Technology I — Adebayo, NOUN](../sources/Industrial-Chemical-Technology-I-NOUN.lingua#r_2026VMVXVJQGK3WTH1P17FE8T9), the complete quantities and unit-operation introductory material; [LearnChemE — chemical-process self-study modules](../sources/LearnChemE-Process-Self-Study.lingua#r_4KM1THP1QA2SZ5JJ9E1HSMN529), all units/dimensions, wet/dry basis and introductory material-balance modules; [Chemical Reactor Analysis and Design Fundamentals — Rawlings and Ekerdt](../sources/Chemical-Reactor-Analysis-Rawlings-Ekerdt.lingua#r_7YQAV27Y3J0B537Z6K5HDN8H0A), the species-amount and composition definitions in the stoichiometry/material-balance chapters.

Use mass, amount of substance, volume, composition and time as distinct quantities. For species i, n_i=m_i/M_i; mole fractions use total amount while mass fractions use total mass. A volume percentage needs a material state and measurement convention. Gas volume comparisons also need pressure, temperature, moisture and the adopted reference basis.

Purity is defined by the quantities actually measured. A main-component assay can coexist with consequential unmeasured trace contaminants, water, particles or degradation products. A dry-basis result must not be substituted into a wet-stream account without converting the total. A nominal concentration can change during storage or transfer.

For an original arithmetic example, 1,000 kg/h of a solution at mass fraction 0.30 contains 300 kg/h of the named solute and 700 kg/h of everything else. If a second stream supplies 200 kg/h at fraction 0.50, the combined solute fraction is 400/1,200=1/3 under complete mixing with no loss or reaction. Volume additivity is an additional assumption, not a consequence of this mass account.

**Work:** reconstruct the bases of a real feed/product specification. Independently convert mass/mole and wet/dry quantities, then compare rated capacity with actual accepted output over a stated period. Preserve uncertainty and any unidentified material.

